      "problem": "Given that $g(t)=5\\cdot {4}^{t},$ find $t$ such that $g(t)=80.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we substitute $g(t)=80$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot {4}^{t}=80\n$$\n\nWe can divide both sides of the equation by the constant factor of $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\cdot {4}^{t}}{5} & =\\frac{80}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}\\cdot {4}^{t}}{\\cancel{5}} & =16 \\\\ {4}^{t} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $t$ using the fact that $16$ is a power of $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{t} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
